from ed import apply
E=[
("> ⚠ **Tool/API caveat.** Package names, matcher setup and option names for axe wrappers (`jest-axe`, `vitest-axe`, `@axe-core/playwright`, `axe-core` itself) change between versions. The code below shows the shape of the API as commonly documented; **verify exact imports and options against each package's current README** before copying. Everything marked *(verify)* falls into this category.",
 "> **Tool/API note (verified 2026-10-22).** Checked by installing and running: `vitest-axe` 0.1.0 (a fork of `jest-axe`, last published January 2025; exports `axe`, `configureAxe`; matchers from `vitest-axe/matchers`; types from `vitest-axe/extend-expect`), `axe-core` 4.14.0, `@axe-core/playwright` 4.14.0 (`AxeBuilder` default export with `withTags`, `include`, `exclude`, `disableRules`, `analyze`; see <https://playwright.dev/docs/accessibility-testing>), and `axe-playwright` 2.2.2 (`injectAxe`, `checkA11y`, `getViolations`, `configureAxe`). `vitest-axe` is lightly maintained; `jest-axe` (4.x) and plain `axe-core` calls wrapped in your own matcher are alternatives. Re-check each package's README when upgrading."),
("WCAG 2.2 is the current W3C Recommendation at the time of writing *(verify the current version at w3.org)*.","WCAG 2.2 (W3C Recommendation, 12 December 2024) is the current version per w3.org, which advises using it; WCAG 2.0 and 2.1 are not deprecated by it (WCAG 3.0 is still a draft)."),
("Tags and their exact names are version-dependent *(verify in axe-core docs)*.","The tags named here are valid in axe-core 4.x (`wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21a`, `wcag21aa`, `best-practice`, ...); the Playwright accessibility guide uses exactly `wcag2a`, `wcag2aa`, `wcag21a`, `wcag21aa`."),
("Elements axe could not decide on (e.g. contrast over a background image) silently land in `incomplete`.","Elements axe could not decide on (e.g. contrast over a background image) silently land in `incomplete`. *Verified with vitest-axe 0.1.0 / axe-core 4.14 in jsdom:* a paragraph with inline `color: #ccc` on white produced **no violation**; `color-contrast` appeared under `incomplete`, so such a test passes `toHaveNoViolations()` while proving nothing about contrast."),
("⚠ In jsdom, \"hidden by CSS\" detection depends","In jsdom, \"hidden by CSS\" detection depends"),
("*(verify how your dialog implementation hides the background)*","(check how your dialog implementation hides the background)"),
("import { axe } from 'vitest-axe';              // (verify) or 'jest-axe' with Jest\nimport * as axeMatchers from 'vitest-axe/matchers'; // (verify)",
 "import { axe } from 'vitest-axe';              // or 'jest-axe' with Jest (verified: vitest-axe 0.1.0 exports `axe` and `configureAxe`)\nimport * as axeMatchers from 'vitest-axe/matchers'; // verified: provides `toHaveNoViolations`"),
("- `axe(container)` runs axe-core against the container subtree. The call is **async** — forgetting `await` makes the test pass vacuously (the promise is never awaited; the assertion is on a pending promise object, which would fail the matcher check, but in some setups produces unhandled rejections after the test ends). Always `await`.",
 "- `axe(container)` runs axe-core against the container subtree. The call is **async**; always `await` it. *Verified with vitest-axe 0.1.0:* `expect(axe(container)).toHaveNoViolations()` without `await` does not pass vacuously; the matcher receives a Promise and throws the misleading error \"No violations found in aXe results object\", and the dangling `axe()` promise keeps running. The test fails, but for a confusing reason."),
("- `expect.extend` registration: type augmentation for `toHaveNoViolations` in TypeScript requires a declaration step per the package's README *(verify)*.","- `expect.extend` registration: for TypeScript, `import 'vitest-axe/extend-expect'` in a `.ts` setup file adds the `toHaveNoViolations` type to Vitest's `expect` (per the package README; the setup file must be included in `tsconfig.json`)."),
("⚠ **jsdom caveat.** At the time of writing, jsdom's support for `HTMLDialogElement.showModal()`, focus trapping, `inert` background handling, and the `close` event is partial or absent *(verify against your jsdom version)*.",
 "**jsdom caveat (verified on jsdom 30.1.3).** `document.createElement('dialog').showModal` and `.close` are `undefined` (calling `showModal()` throws `showModal is not a function`), and the `inert` property is not reflected; focus trapping and `:modal` handling are therefore absent too."),
("(⚠ the first Tab target depends on","(the first Tab target depends on"),
("that only make sense on full pages *(verify rule ids)*;","that only make sense on full pages (verified: scanning `document.body` of a bare fragment reported `region`, and `configureAxe({ rules: { region: { enabled: false } } })` removed it; scanning the RTL `container` did not report it);"),
("import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright'; // (verify import style for your version)","import AxeBuilder from '@axe-core/playwright'; // default export (verified in the Playwright a11y guide and by type-checking against 4.14)"),
("      .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa', 'wcag21a', 'wcag21aa']) // (verify tag names)","      .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa', 'wcag21a', 'wcag21aa']) // tag names verified in the Playwright a11y guide"),
("**B3.** Without `await`, `expect(promise)` receives a Promise, not results; `toHaveNoViolations` would evaluate against the wrong value (it may throw an error about the received value, or, depending on setup/linting, the test body may finish before the check runs and failures are lost as unhandled rejections). Either way the test is unreliable.",
 "**B3.** Without `await`, `expect(promise)` receives a Promise, not results; `toHaveNoViolations` evaluates the wrong value (verified with vitest-axe 0.1.0: it throws \"No violations found in aXe results object\", so the test fails with a misleading message and the real scan is abandoned). With other matcher libraries the check may silently never run. Either way the test is unreliable."),
]
